# Table Column Ordinals

Column **display order** is the order of entries in the React column definition (array order or the order columns are declared in JSX). There is no separate numeric ordinal field.

## When adding a column

1. Insert the column definition at the intended index in the column list (or between sibling column components).
2. Ensure headers, cells, and any width/accessor keys stay aligned with that single ordered list.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — column added only in the header row, not in the body cell list
const columns = [
  { id: "name", header: "Name", cell: NameCell },
  { id: "status", header: "Status", cell: StatusCell },
];
// ...tbody maps a different set of cells

// ✅ GOOD — one ordered list drives header and body
const columns = [
  { id: "name", header: "Name", cell: NameCell },
  { id: "segment", header: "Segment", cell: SegmentCell },
  { id: "amount", header: "Amount", cell: AmountCell },
];
```

## When removing or moving a column

Update the same column definition list: remove the entry or reorder the array. Keep filter/sort keys in sync if the table uses them.

## Do not

- Maintain a duplicate ordering mechanism that can drift from the React column list.
- Add a new column in one file without updating the paired column definition used for rendering.
